// =========================================================================
// dlcd-revocation.spthy — verified revocation for time/epoch-bounded credentials
// =========================================================================
//
// Phase 4 of the agent-governance program (spec/revocation-design.md). The
// field's named gap: TTL-based expiry fails at agent execution speed, so a
// leaked/rogue credential stays live for the whole TTL window ("short-lived
// credentials are necessary but not sufficient", nhimg 2026). The research
// target is FAST revocation whose protocol is formally mechanized — named OPEN
// by "Heartbeat-Bound Hierarchical Credentials: Cryptographic Revocation for AI
// Agent Swarms" (arXiv 2605.20704). This theory is that mechanization for the
// DLC-D revocable credential (`within validUntil (p says φ)`; the Lean side is
// `lean/DLC/Revocation.lean`).
//
// MODEL-FIRST (CLAUDE.md): Tamarin FIRST, then ProVerif (dlcd-revocation.pv, next
// increment), then wire. House style of dlcd-interop.spthy: builtins signing; the
// Equality restriction discharges signature checks; long-term-key reveal
// conditions the forgery-resistance lemma; an exists-trace witness rules out
// vacuity; a differential BITE (dlcd-revocation-bite.spthy) proves the revocation
// check is load-bearing.
//
// Modelling choices:
//   1. A credential is `<'cred', I, cid, sign(<'cred', I, cid>, skI)>` — issuer
//      `I`, a fresh credential id `~cid` (names exactly one issuance), signed by
//      `I`. This is the wire form of `Says(I, φ)` carrying an identifier the
//      revocation list keys on.
//   2. Tamarin has no native time; an EPOCH is the trace ordering (`#r < #a`) and
//      REVOCATION is a `Revoked(I, cid)` action published by the issuer. The
//      verifier consults the revocation list: it accepts only if NO revocation of
//      that credential precedes the acceptance — modelled by the `RevocationCheck`
//      restriction (the standard Tamarin idiom; V2X revocation, NDSS 2024). This
//      is exactly the `now < τ` / not-revoked gate that DLC's `within-E` defers to
//      `dlc-crypto`, and that `Revocation.lean`'s `AcceptsRevocable` enforces at
//      the Lean level. Removing this restriction is the BITE.

theory DLCD_Revocation
begin

builtins: signing

// =========================================================================
// Restrictions
// =========================================================================

// Signature verification, the derivable way (the Verify rule emits the check as
// an action; this restriction discharges it).
restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

// One keypair per principal — otherwise "its" signature is not well defined.
restriction unique_key:
  "All A #i #j.
      KeyRegistered(A) @ #i & KeyRegistered(A) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

// THE structural heart of revocation: a verifier accepts a credential only if NO
// revocation of it was published BEFORE the acceptance. This models the verifier
// consulting the revocation list / heartbeat (the `now < τ` + not-revoked gate).
// Removing this is the bite (dlcd-revocation-bite.spthy) — post-revocation replay
// becomes reachable.
restriction RevocationCheck:
  "All I cid #a.
      Accept(I, cid) @ #a
    ==>
      not (Ex #r. Revoked(I, cid) @ #r & #r < #a)"

// =========================================================================
// Keys and compromise
// =========================================================================

rule Register_key:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ HonestKey($A, ~sk), KeyRegistered($A) ]->
    [ !Ltk($A, ~sk), !Pk($A, pk(~sk)), Out(pk(~sk)) ]

// Static long-term-key reveal — the adversary learns a principal's secret. The
// forgery-resistance lemma is conditioned on this not having happened.
rule Reveal_ltk:
    [ !Ltk($A, sk) ]
  --[ Reveal($A) ]->
    [ Out(sk) ]

// =========================================================================
// The protocol
// =========================================================================

// An issuer mints a says-credential with a fresh id `~cid` (the revocation list
// keys on it). Wire form of `Says(I, φ)`.
rule Issue_cred:
    [ Fr(~cid), !Ltk($I, skI) ]
  --[ CredIssued($I, ~cid) ]->
    [ Out(<'cred', $I, ~cid, sign(<'cred', $I, ~cid>, skI)>) ]

// The issuer REVOKES a credential: it publishes a signed revocation for `cid`.
// This is the heartbeat/revocation-list update. Signed by the issuer's key, so
// only the issuer (or a revealed key) can revoke its own credentials.
rule Revoke_cred:
    [ In(<'cred', $I, cid, credsig>), !Ltk($I, skI) ]
  --[ Revoked($I, cid) ]->
    [ Out(<'revoke', $I, cid, sign(<'revoke', $I, cid>, skI)>) ]

// A verifier accepts a credential: it checks the issuer's signature over the
// exact body. The `RevocationCheck` restriction ADDITIONALLY forbids acceptance
// once the credential has been revoked (the verifier's revocation-list lookup).
rule Verify_accept:
    [ In(<'cred', I, cid, sig>), !Pk(I, pkI) ]
  --[ Eq(verify(sig, <'cred', I, cid>, pkI), true)
    , Accept(I, cid) ]->
    [ ]

// =========================================================================
// Security lemmas (all-traces) + non-vacuity witness (exists-trace)
// =========================================================================

// FORGERY-RESISTANCE. An accepted credential was really issued by the issuer it
// names — unless that issuer's key was revealed. (The revocation machinery does
// not weaken this; it is the wire form of `verify_in_keyring`.)
lemma accept_binds_issuer:
  all-traces
  "All I cid #a.
       Accept(I, cid) @ #a
    ==>
       (Ex #j. CredIssued(I, cid) @ #j)
     | (Ex #r. Reveal(I) @ #r)"

// REVOCATION SOUNDNESS. An accepted credential was NOT revoked before the moment
// of acceptance — the `Deriv`-level `revoked_credential_not_accepted`
// (Revocation.lean) at the protocol layer. Enabled by `RevocationCheck`; the
// bite proves that restriction is what buys this.
lemma accept_not_revoked:
  all-traces
  "All I cid #a.
       Accept(I, cid) @ #a
    ==>
       not (Ex #r. Revoked(I, cid) @ #r & #r < #a)"

// PERMANENCE (no un-revocation). If a credential is both revoked and accepted,
// the acceptance strictly PRECEDES the revocation — once revoked, it is never
// accepted again. The protocol twin of `revoked_stays_revoked` (Revocation.lean).
lemma revoke_is_permanent:
  all-traces
  "All I cid #r #a.
       Revoked(I, cid) @ #r & Accept(I, cid) @ #a
    ==>
       #a < #r"

// NON-VACUITY (anti-vacuity witness). The safety lemmas above are not vacuously
// true: there is a trace where an honest credential is issued and accepted with
// no key ever revealed (so `accept_*` is not holding merely because nothing is
// accepted).
lemma exec_accept:
  exists-trace
  "Ex I cid #a #j.
       CredIssued(I, cid) @ #j
     & Accept(I, cid) @ #a
     & not (Ex X #r. Reveal(X) @ #r)"

// =========================================================================
// Fences (next increments — NOT yet modelled)
// =========================================================================
//   * ProVerif cross-check (`models/proverif/dlcd-revocation.pv`) — next.
//   * The revocation LIST is an issuer-published action; a distributed/quorum
//     revocation service (multiple revokers, gossip) is a later refinement.
//   * Epoch is trace ordering here; a numeric epoch/bound with arithmetic
//     comparison is the Lean model's job (`acceptableAt`, Revocation.lean).

end
